<p>
Over at <a href="https://emacs.dyerdwelling.family/emacs/20260515110240-emacs--that-moment-dired-eats-your-file-and-why-trash-saves-you/">Emacs Dwelling</a> there&rsquo;s a nice post on <a href="https://emacs.dyerdwelling.family/emacs/20260515110240-emacs--that-moment-dired-eats-your-file-and-why-trash-saves-you/">why you should set Emacs to delete files by moving them to trash</a>. That&rsquo;s easy to do by setting <code>delete-by-moving-to-trash</code> to <code>t</code>. The post has a story illustrating why you should do that: it&rsquo;s very easy to accidentally delete a file with Dired and if you do it to a file with changes that haven&rsquo;t been committed to version control you can lose a few hours work. If you don&rsquo;t use version control, the story is even worse.
</p>
<p>
The post seemed to me to be a worthy cautionary tale and I thought it would make a good Irreal post but when I began the writing process by entering a file name for the post, <code>delete-to-trash.org</code>, I discovered that such a file already existed from 2022. Even worse, for my blogging plans, <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=10817">it said everything I was planning to say in this post</a>. Nevertheless, that was 4 years ago and the lesson, it seems, is evergreen so it&rsquo;s worth making the point again. It costs you nothing and in those rare occasions where errant muscle memory causes you to delete a file, it can save the day. So rather than repeat the story I&rsquo;ll just suggest that you check out the Emacs Dwelling post or my previous effort.</p>
